plugin-hostd-protocol (0.4.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The protocol page and protocol.h say how a pin is keyed: by its clap:
    uri's path decoded, so the uri written with the space as it is and
    written encoded name one pin; '%' is always an escape, and a name
    holding one writes it %25.

 -- Pau Aliagas <linuxnow@gmail.com>  Fri, 06 Nov 2026 21:00:00 +0100

plugin-hostd-protocol (0.4.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A path of a pin, and the path of a clap: uri, is written percent-encoded:
    a separator of pin_set, whitespace, '%', a quote, a backslash and a
    control byte are %XX, so any file name is one word. protocol.h declares
    the escape and phd_pin_path_encode() and phd_pin_path_decode().
  * add: a clap: uri whose path holds a space and is written bare is one
    uri; phd_add_uri() is that reading, for every host that answers add.
  * phd_headless_qt(): with no display, a host gives Qt the offscreen
    platform unless the user set one, so a Qt-linked plugin (synthv1) does
    not abort as it is instantiated.

 -- Pau Aliagas <linuxnow@gmail.com>  Fri, 06 Nov 2026 17:00:00 +0100

plugin-hostd-protocol (0.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * lv2_worker auto is no longer a word of the protocol: a plugin the chosen
    LV2 worker refuses is answered with its code and never handed to another
    host. lv2_worker and lv2_worker_set take mod-host or omx-clap-host, and
    lv2_adapter_refused names omx-clap-host only.
  * pin_accept of a plugin whose pin carries verdict=refused (pins_dir or
    pin_file) is the user's choice to load it anyway, alone in a worker of
    its own; pin_clear takes it back; -516 is no longer its answer.
  * plugin_db_vocabulary.h is the render of plugin-db 0.2.0's words.

 -- Pau Aliagas <linuxnow@gmail.com>  Fri, 06 Nov 2026 01:30:00 +0100

plugin-hostd-protocol (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * hello: a client asks for the protocol version and the extensions it
    wants, and a host that calls protocol_add_hello() answers with the
    declared ones it grants. A client that never sends hello, or whose hello
    is refused, is answered as mod-host answers it.
  * Request tags: a session granted them sends "@<tag> " before a command
    and gets the tag back on the answer.
  * Error words: in a session granted them, a refusal "resp <n>" carries
    the code the protocol declares for n.
  * Event sequence numbers: in a session granted them, every feedback line
    starts with "#<n> ", so a client sees a line it missed.
  * param_get_many and param_set_many: several values of one instance in one
    line. param_set_many checks every pair before it sets any and sets
    nothing when one is refused.
  * The default lv2_worker is omx-clap-host. mod-host runs an LV2 plugin only
    when lv2_worker or lv2_worker_set names it (#13).
  * worker_list and plan: a worker is pinned to the whole set of audio cores,
    so the core field reads - (#15).
  * A track_info name is held to UTF-8 and refuses the C1 control characters
    (#11). A word "..." where a pattern ends in "..." is read as an argument
    (#12).
  * Fuzz harnesses for the parser and the session, with their records,
    sabotage arms and a coverage floor, run in CI.
  * The packages install their licence and a short README and no other
    markdown; their descriptions point to openmixer.org.
  * protocol_add_hello() and host_scenario_set_host() are the exported symbols
    added; nothing is removed.
  * The words of a pin in protocol.h are the ones plugin-db declares
    (plugin_db_vocabulary.h, rendered from plugin-db 0.1.0's package of the
    contract directory).
  * Dependencies carry a floor and a ceiling: omx-contract >= 3.9.0 and
    < 4 to build, >= 3.2.0 and < 4 to install the contract package.

 -- Pau Aliagas <linuxnow@gmail.com>  Wed, 04 Nov 2026 18:00:00 +0100

plugin-hostd-protocol (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The whole declared protocol moves here from plugin-hostd:
    plugin-hostd/protocol.h and the headers omx-contract renders for it
    (plugin_hostd_protocol_host_verbs.h, plugin_hostd_protocol_vocabulary.h,
    omx_clap_core_host_verbs.h, omx_clap_core_vocabulary.h), data/verbs.json,
    protocol/plugin-hostd.json and its schema, protocol/mod-host.json, and
    the generators and checks that keep them current.
  * One verb table, protocol/plugin-hostd-protocol.json: every command, each
    once, with its origin, its argument types, its reply and its refusals,
    and every feedback line. A word of ours that changes a mod-host word is
    refused.
  * The grammar of a line in ABNF, generated from the table, and
    doc/protocol.md, generated from the table and the declarations; the
    scenario corpus and every reply of the golden transcript are held to the
    grammar.
  * New package plugin-hostd-protocol-contract: data/verbs.json as the
    package plugin-hostd-protocol of omx-contract's contract directory.
  * The library package installs the table, the grammar and the JSON of the
    protocol and of mod-host's, which programs read at run time; the
    development package installs the headers under plugin-hostd/, the
    schema and the generator's source. pkg-config variables name each
    (protocoldir, table, grammar, protocoljson, protocolschema, modhostjson,
    protocolgen).
  * The library's exported symbols and types are those of 0.1.0.
  * protocol_parse() reads every form registered for a verb and runs the first
    whose arguments fit in number and type, so both forms of preset_show
    dispatch through the library (#5).

 -- Pau Aliagas <linuxnow@gmail.com>  Tue, 03 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100

plugin-hostd-protocol (0.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First release: mod-host's socket server, line protocol and command
    dispatch (FreeMixer/mod-host 013ac7d, the code of mod-host-protocol
    0.10.6-11.3) as a library of its own, the same API under the name
    plugin-hostd-protocol.
  * param_info writes NaN, Infinity and -Infinity for a value that is not
    finite, where it wrote past a stack array (plugin-hostd#371).
  * A command whose first word is not a verb's no longer runs that verb's
    callback (plugin-hostd#372).

 -- Pau Aliagas <linuxnow@gmail.com>  Mon, 02 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100
